## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the integration of fundamental data structures—arrays, linked lists, hashmaps, and heaps—to solve a streaming scenario with sliding window deduplication and real-time ranking.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window deduplication per (user_id, action) using a hashmap with timestamp tracking
- Per-user score aggregation maintained in a hashmap for O(1) updates
- Global top-K ranking via max-heap with O(log K) insertion/deletion and lazy invalidation
- Efficient expiration handling using linked lists and amortized cleanup strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class EventProcessor {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.latestByUserAction = new Map(); // (user_id,action) -> timestamp
    this.userTotals = new Map(); // user_id -> total_score
    this.userEvents = new Map(); // user_id -> linked list of (timestamp, score)
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap(); // (total_score, user_id)
  }

  addEvent(event) {
    const key = `${event.user_id}:${event.action}`;
    const latest = this.latestByUserAction.get(key);

    if (!latest || event.ts - latest > this.W) {
      // Update dedup map
      this.latestByUserAction.set(key, event.ts);

      // Update user total
      const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(event.user_id) || 0;
      const newTotal = currentTotal + event.score;
      this.userTotals.set(event.user_id, newTotal);

      // Add to user's event list
      if (!this.userEvents.has(event.user_id)) {
        this.userEvents.set(event.user_id, new LinkedList());
      }
      this.userEvents.get(event.user_id).push({ts: event.ts, score: event.score});

      // Update heap
      this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: event.user_id});
    }
  }

  getTopK(k, now) {
    this.expire(now);
    return this.maxHeap.getTopK(k);
  }

  expire(now) {
    for (const [userId, events] of this.userEvents) {
      while (events.length > 0 && now - events.head.ts > this.W) {
        const expired = events.shift();
        const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId);
        const newTotal = currentTotal - expired.score;
        this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);

        if (newTotal > 0) {
          this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: userId});
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
**Time:** O(1) amortized per event for updates, O(log K) for top-K retrieval
**Space:** O(N) where N is number of active users plus events within window W

